Stop PyNaCl from building its own bundled libsodium, so the cffi bindings
link the cross-compiled one that flet-libsodium provides.

PyNaCl vendors the entire libsodium source tree under `src/libsodium` and, by
default, builds it as part of the wheel. `setup.py` wires that up with the
two things this patch comments out:

  * `distclass=Distribution` — a subclass whose `has_c_libraries()` returns
    True unless `SODIUM_INSTALL=system`, which is what enables the C-library
    build at all;
  * `cmdclass={"build_clib": build_clib, "build_ext": build_ext}` — a
    `build_clib` that chmods and runs the vendored `./configure`, then `make`,
    `make check` and `make install`, and a `build_ext` that prepends the
    resulting `include/`, `lib/` and `lib64/` to the extension's search paths.

None of that survives a cross-compile:

  * the vendored `configure` is invoked with no `--host`, so autoconf probes
    the machine doing the build — its compile/link/run tests either answer
    for macOS or cannot run at all under the iOS/Android toolchain;
  * `make check` builds and *runs* libsodium's test suite, which is impossible
    when the binaries target another architecture;
  * and even a libsodium that somehow built would be the wrong one — the
    recipe already pulls a properly cross-compiled libsodium via
    `requirements.host: flet-libsodium`.

With both lines commented out, stock setuptools commands are used:
`has_c_libraries()` is False, no `build_clib` runs, and `build_ext` adds no
bundled paths. `cffi_modules=["src/bindings/build.py:ffi"]` still runs, and
its `ffi.set_source("_sodium", …, libraries=["sodium"])` compiles
`#include <sodium.h>` and links `-lsodium` — resolved against the
`opt/include` / `opt/lib` that the flet-libsodium wheel unpacks into the cross
environment (forge adds that install root to the compile env as
`-I<site-packages>/opt/include` and `-L<site-packages>/opt/lib`).
On iOS that resolves to
flet-libsodium's static `libsodium.a`, absorbed into `nacl/_sodium…so`; on
Android it resolves to the shared `libsodium.so`, which the app build stages
into `jniLibs/<abi>/`.

Without the patch the build dies inside `build_clib`, long before the
bindings are compiled.

Worth knowing: upstream exposes the same end state through an environment
variable — `SODIUM_INSTALL=system` makes `use_system()` return True, which
short-circuits `build_clib.run()` and flips `has_c_libraries()` to False, so
`build.script_env` could have carried this instead of a patch. The commits
that introduced the patch (PR #29, "PyNaCl for iOS and Android") record no
reasoning, so why the file was patched rather than the variable set is not
known; the two are equivalent as far as this build is concerned.

One cosmetic wart: the replacement comment names only `build_ext`, while the
line it replaces also registered `build_clib`. It is a comment, so nothing
depends on it.

diff --git a/setup.py b/setup.py
--- a/setup.py
+++ b/setup.py
@@ -197,7 +197,7 @@ setup(
     package_data={"nacl": ["py.typed"]},
     ext_package="nacl",
     cffi_modules=["src/bindings/build.py:ffi"],
-    cmdclass={"build_clib": build_clib, "build_ext": build_ext},
-    distclass=Distribution,
+    # cmdclass={"build_ext": build_ext},
+    # distclass=Distribution,
     zip_safe=False,
 )
